The recent trend is not what blocks it this time. ROIC is flat year on year and ROE has stopped falling (11.3% TTM against 11.0% in FY2025), so it looks more like a trough than a continuing slide. The stock is also cheap: P/E and P/B sit below their 5-year lows, and the price is under even the mechanical bear fair value of 104.79 TWD.

What blocks it is missing evidence. ROE halved over five years, and the fact sheet has no credit costs, margins or leverage for this lender. China exposure is also unsized, so I can't tell a temporary credit-cycle dip from a business that now earns permanently lower returns. Calling that conviction 4 would be inflating it.

A lower price wouldn't change that, which is why the entry price is null. The Nov 12 results, or any credit-cost disclosure, should trigger a re-research.
